14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2010 Number 466);  
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 42);  
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 903);  
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2022 concerning Service Standards for the Protection of Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2022 Number 85);  

With Mutual Approval

# REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF KATINGAN REGENCY

# REGENT OF KATINGAN

# HAS DECIDED:

To Establish: REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

# CHAPTER I

## GENERAL PROVISIONS

#### Article 1

In this Regional Regulation, what is meant by:

1. Region is Katingan Regency.  
2. Regional Government is the Government of Katingan Regency.  
3. Regent is the Regent of Katingan.  
4. Regional House of Representatives hereinafter referred to as DPRD is the Regional House of Representatives of Katingan Regency.  
5. Protection is all efforts aimed at providing a sense of security to victims carried out by family, advocates, social institutions, police, prosecutors, courts, or other parties either temporarily or based on court determination.  
6. Woman is an adult human being of female sex and a person recognized by law as a woman.  
7. Child is a person who has not reached 18 years of age, including a Child in the womb.  
8. Violence is any act that results in or causes misery and suffering whether physical, sexual, psychological including neglect, threat of certain actions, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, whether occurring in public and or in private life.  
9. Violence against children is any act against a child that results in misery or suffering physically, mentally sexually, psychologically, including neglect and ill-treatment that threatens the integrity and degrades the dignity of the child.  

4 -

10. Violence against women is any action or may result in misery or suffering to women physically, mentally sexually or psychologically including threats of certain actions, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, whether occurring in the public sphere or private life.  
11. Victims of violence against women and children are individuals or groups, who experience misery and or suffering as a result of violence and or threat of violence.  
12. Protection of women is all activities aimed at providing a sense of security carried out by the police, prosecutors, courts, social institutions, or other parties who know or hear that violence against women will or has occurred.  
13. Child protection is all activities to guarantee and protect children and their rights so they can live, grow, develop and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and receive protection from violence and discrimination.  
14. Service is action taken as soon as possible to victims when seeing, hearing and knowing that violence against women and children will, is or has occurred.  
15. Companion is a person or representative from an institution/organization/agency providing services to victims of violence against women and children who are trained or have expertise in accordance with their competence and authority in providing assistance to victims of violence in accordance with the type of service provided.  
16. Social rehabilitation is a service aimed at restoring and developing the ability of a person experiencing social dysfunction so that they can carry out their social functions properly.  
17. Social reintegration is an effort to reunite victims with family, substitute family, or community that can provide protection and fulfillment of needs for victims.  
18. Legal Aid is legal services provided by companions and/or legal apparatus which includes providing legal consultation, exercising power of attorney, representing, accompanying, defending and taking other legal actions for victims of violence in accordance with their competence and authority.  
19. Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children hereinafter abbreviated as UPTD PPA is a functional technical implementation unit as the organizer of integrated services for women and children who experience violence discrimination and other problems.  
20. Standard Operating Procedures hereinafter abbreviated as SOP are standard steps that must be taken in protecting victims starting from handling complaints, health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, legal aid, recovery/psychological services, mediation to repatriation and social reintegration.  
21. Minimum Service Standards abbreviated as SPM are performance benchmarks for UPTD PPA services in providing complaint handling services, health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, legal aid, recovery/psychological services, mediation to repatriation and social reintegration.  
22. Social Protection House is a temporary residence, provided to give protection to victims in accordance with predetermined standards.

# CHAPTER II

## PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE OF REGULATION

#### Article 2

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence, is implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;  

5 -

b. justice and gender equality;  
c. non-discrimination;  
d. best interests of victims;  
e. local wisdom;  
f. right to life, survival and development of children;  
g. respect for victims' opinions;  
h. legal certainty.

#### Article 3

Protection of Women and Children victims of violence aims to:

a. prevent all forms of violence against women and children;  
b. protect women and children and provide services to women and Children victims of violence;  
c. empower women and Children victims of violence.

#### Article 4

The Scope of Regulation on Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence includes:

a. victims' rights;  
b. authority, obligations and responsibilities;  
c. institutions;  
d. minimum service standards;  
e. social protection house;  
f. reporting;  
g. guidance and supervision;  
h. community participation;  
i. funding.

# CHAPTER III

## VICTIMS' RIGHTS

#### Article 5

Every Woman and Child victim of violence has the right:

a. to have their dignity respected as a human being;  
b. to receive protection from family, community, Regional Government and other parties either temporarily or based on a Protection Order from the Court;  
c. to receive recovery health services physically, psychologically or sexually according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;  
d. to receive special handling related to victim confidentiality;  
e. to receive assistance from companions and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;  
f. to receive spiritual guidance services.

#### Article 6

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 5, also receive special rights as follows:

a. respect for survival, growth and development;  
b. to education, health and access to parents during the handling process; danc. to receive equal protection related to status, citizenship, race, skin color, gender, language, religion, politics, disability, birth or other status.  

6 -

# CHAPTER IV

## AUTHORITY, OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

#### Article 7

1. The Regional Government has the authority and responsibility for the Protection of Women and Children victims of violence.  
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
   a. guaranteeing the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with laws and regulations;  
   b. implementing policies for the protection of Women and Children victims of violence based on laws and regulations;  
   c. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of Women and Children victims of violence;  
   d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;  
   e. allocating budget in the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;  
   f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;  
   g. improving the quality and competence of human resources who provide services/assistance for victims of violence against women and children;  
   h. cooperating with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of protection of women in accordance with laws and regulations;  
   i. taking preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children.  
3. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women and children as referred to in Article 7 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

#### Article 8

1. The community may participate and be responsible in services, prevention, assistance, recovery and monitoring of victims of violence.  
2. Community participation and responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in the form of:
   a. preventing acts of violence against Women and Children;  
   b. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against Women and Children to the competent authorities;  
   c. providing emergency assistance; and  
   d. providing protection to victims of violence.

# CHAPTER V

## INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

#### Article 9

1. The established UPTD PPA may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as safe houses, social protection houses for Children, trauma center protection houses, women's social protection houses, shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable Laws and Regulations.  
2. Forms of services implemented by UPTD PPA include:
   a. handling complaints;  
   b. health rehabilitation;  
   c. social rehabilitation;  
   d. legal assistance;  
   e. recovery/psychological services;  
   f. mediation; and  
   g. repatriation and social reintegration.  
3. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with SPM.

# CHAPTER VI

## MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS

#### Article 10

1. SPM is implemented by the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children with the aim of ensuring that Women and Children victims of violence receive the minimum services needed.  
2. SPM as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:
   a. handling complaints/reports of victims of violence against women and children;  
   b. health rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;  
   c. social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;  
   d. legal assistance for women and children victims of violence;  
   e. recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;  
   f. mediation for women and children victims of violence; and  
   g. repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of violence.

#### Article 11

1. SPM as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (3) has performance indicators including:
   a. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within UPTD PPA;  
   b. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;  
   c. coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for Women and Children victims of violence within UPTD PPA;  
   d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children victims of violence within UPTD PPA;  
   e. coverage of legal assistance services for women and children victims of violence;  
   f. coverage of recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;  
   g. coverage of mediation services for women and children victims of violence;  
   h. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; and  
   i. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.  
2. The determination of SPM UPTD PPA performance indicators for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes the minimum target that must be achieved.

# CHAPTER VII

## SOCIAL PROTECTION HOUSES

#### Article 12

1. The Regional Government is obliged to establish Social Protection Houses and/or Safe Houses.  
2. Social Protection Houses and/or Safe Houses as referred to in paragraph (1) are to ensure the implementation of UPTD PPA services.  
3. For the implementation of services as referred to in paragraph (2), the Regional Government may utilize social protection houses and/or Safe Houses owned by the community or other social service institutions.

# CHAPTER VIII

## REPORTING

#### Article 13

1. The Regent is responsible for making reports on the implementation of SPM through UPTD PPA, submitted to the Governor with copies submitted to the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection and the Minister of Home Affairs.  
2. Reporting of implementation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted annually.  
3. The form of reporting as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER IX

## GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

#### Article 14

1. The Regent conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of UPTD PPA. 
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
   a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;  
   b. technical guidance and training;  
   c. supervision;  
   d. provision of facilities;  
   e. monitoring; and  
   f. evaluation.  
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is in order to realize the objectives of protection of women and Children victims of violence in accordance with minimum service standards.

# CHAPTER X

## COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

#### Article 15

1. In implementing protection for Women and Children victims of violence, the community may:
   a. form family partners at the village and sub-district level coordinating with Damang or Mantir Adat and Community Leaders;  
   b. form women and child protection units within community organizations;  
   c. conduct socialization of the rights of Women and Children independently;  
   d. provide first aid to victims; and  
   e. report to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.  
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, family partners, community social institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

# CHAPTER XI

## FUNDING

#### Article 16

* \ Funds for the implementation of protection for Women and Children victims of violence, are sourced from: - 9 -
a. State Budgetb. Regional Budget; andc. other legitimate and non-binding sources in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER XII

## CLOSING PROVISIONS

#### Article 17

This Regional Regulation shall come into force on the date of promulgation.  

So that everyone may know of it, orders the promulgation of this Regional Regulation by placing it in the Regional Gazette of Katingan Regency.  

